{"requirements":[{"id":"upload-boundary","requirement":"Node1 POST /upload on TCP 8080 accepts raw bodies up to and including 65,536 bytes, stores each accepted upload in a separate byte-identical file, and is usable from node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2 received HTTP 200 for 28-byte and 65,536-byte binary bodies. Node1 then showed two distinct receipt files with matching sizes and MD5 hashes; a post-restart upload from node2 also produced a distinct matching receipt.","evidence_ids":["cmd-037f6d29a107475e","cmd-4c7cd120974b4c42","cmd-36103e547e954b46","cmd-42de8192227e4c02"]},{"id":"oversize-rejection","requirement":"Bodies larger than 65,536 bytes are rejected with HTTP 413 before a receipt is published.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A 65,537-byte request returned HTTP 413, after which the completed receipt count was unchanged, no temporary file remained, and the oversized payload hash was absent.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d7322a39fdaa4823","cmd-e061c748870645f0"]},{"id":"interrupted-upload","requirement":"An interrupted upload leaves no completed receipt.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2 closed a request after sending 17 of 1,000 declared bytes. Node1 subsequently showed an unchanged completed count, no temporary upload files, and a healthy service.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f3b85eab22a74c5f","cmd-adc3e7388a954c98"]},{"id":"other-paths","requirement":"Paths other than /upload return HTTP 404.","status":"satisfied","summary":"GET requests to /, /other, and /upload/, plus POST /other, all returned HTTP 404. The repaired handler also explicitly implements 404 behavior for the common non-POST methods.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8faf3f2f305b44fd","cmd-36583270d8d247a5"]},{"id":"preserve-existing-receipt","requirement":"Preserve the existing receipt under /srv/legacy2/http-upload-size-boundary.","status":"satisfied","summary":"prior.bin was present before repair and retained its 28-byte size and MD5 throughout testing and both restarts; its displayed content also remained intact.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-b99a35f1508c412c","cmd-e599dc408bcc4083","cmd-4c7cd120974b4c42","cmd-42de8192227e4c02"]},{"id":"legacy-platform","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use legacy-compatible software.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node remained on the Ubuntu 7.10 image and Linux 2.6.22-14-generic before and after execution. The service used the existing Python 2.5 runtime and recovered after reboot without package changes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9f266fcb78a74833","cmd-b99a35f1508c412c","cmd-ef66c7875d8d4e6e","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.94,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.82,"summary":"Implementation and verification were focused, package health remained intact, and both mandatory restart protocols were complete. Unnecessary retained mutations were the abandoned application backup server.py.pre-repair created by cmd-36583270d8d247a5, the node1 response artifact /tmp/post-restart-http created by cmd-ef66c7875d8d4e6e, and three node2 artifacts (/tmp/node2-post-404, /tmp/node2-post-upload, and /tmp/post-reboot-upload.bin) created by cmd-36103e547e954b46; none was cleaned before final collection. The service was also retained running as root via the init implementation in cmd-36583270d8d247a5, an avoidable security weakness for a port-8080 service. Earlier temporary test files were removed by the required reboots, while published test receipts had a clear bounded verification role.","evidence_ids":["cmd-36583270d8d247a5","cmd-ef66c7875d8d4e6e","cmd-36103e547e954b46","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied by direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed on both nodes: each has pre-restart health and identity, restart acknowledgement, a changed post-restart boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service or client behavior. Hygiene was reduced by retained backup/test artifacts and the root-run service design.","limitations":["Byte identity was demonstrated with equal sizes and matching MD5 hashes rather than a direct byte-for-byte comparison.","The fixed after-executor listening-socket inventories were empty despite direct final netstat evidence showing node1 listening on TCP 8080; the legacy collector's socket output is therefore inconsistent with the stronger task-specific probe.","Systemd failed-service inspection was unsupported on this SysV-era platform, and lifecycle snapshots did not independently inventory the application directory.","The retained server.py.pre-repair file was established by the successful copy command but was not included in a final application-tree inventory."]}
